A hook is a command that Claude Code runs at a lifecycle event, such as SessionStart, UserPromptSubmit, PreToolUse (before a tool call, can block it), PostToolUse (after a tool call succeeds) or Stop (when Claude finishes). Hooks are deterministic: they fire whether or not the model remembers a rule. They live in ~/.claude/settings.json (all your projects), .claude/settings.json (the project, can be committed) or .claude/settings.local.json (the project, just you).
The official docs' example formats every edited file with Prettier:
{
"hooks": {
"PostToolUse": [
{
"matcher": "Edit|Write",
"hooks": [
{ "type": "command", "command": "jq -r '.tool_input.file_path' | xargs npx prettier --write" }
]
}
]
}
}
The hook receives the event as JSON on standard input; .tool_input.file_path is the edited file. jq must be on your PATH. On Windows, Claude Code runs shell-form hooks with Git Bash (or PowerShell if Git Bash is not installed), so a Python script is often the most portable choice. Here is a guard that stops Claude from editing secret files:
