import json
import sys
event = json.load(sys.stdin) # Claude Code sends the event as JSON
path = event.get("tool_input", {}).get("file_path", "").replace("\\", "/")
name = path.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
if name == ".env" or name.startswith(".env.") or name.endswith((".pem", ".key")):
print(f"Blocked: {path} holds secrets. Ask the user to edit it by hand.", file=sys.stderr)
sys.exit(2) # 2 = block; stderr goes back to Claude
sys.exit(0)
{
"hooks": {
"PreToolUse": [
{
"matcher": "Edit|Write",
"hooks": [
{ "type": "command", "command": "python \"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR\"/.claude/hooks/protect_secrets.py" }
]
}
]
}
}
Common pitfall: exiting with code 1 to "fail". For most events, only exit code 2 blocks; exit 1 is a non-blocking error and the action goes ahead. And PostToolUse cannot block at all, because the tool already ran.

Modes set the baseline. Rules add exact allow, ask and deny lists in the same settings files as hooks. Rules are checked in the order deny, then ask, then allow, and the first match wins, so a broad deny always beats a narrow allow.
{
"permissions": {
"allow": ["Bash(npm run test *)", "Bash(git status)", "Bash(git diff *)"],
"ask": ["Bash(git commit *)"],
"deny": ["Bash(git push *)", "Read(./.env)", "Read(./secrets/**)"]
}
}
Watch out: a Bash rule matches the command as written. The docs note that git -C . push does not match Bash(git push *). Treat rules as a strong guard rail, not a sandbox. For real isolation, use Claude Code's sandbox, a container or a VM.

Write a PostToolUse hook that runs ruff format on the edited file, only for .py files, and does nothing if ruff is not installed. Register it in .claude/settings.json.
Solution
# .claude/hooks/format_python.py - PostToolUse hook: format each edited Python file
import json
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
event = json.load(sys.stdin)
path = event.get("tool_input", {}).get("file_path", "")
if path.endswith(".py") and shutil.which("ruff"):
done = subprocess.run(["ruff", "format", path], capture_output=True, text=True)
if done.returncode != 0:
print(done.stderr or "ruff format failed", file=sys.stderr)
sys.exit(1) # non-blocking: shown as a hook error
{
"hooks": {
"PostToolUse": [
{
"matcher": "Edit|Write",
"hooks": [
{ "type": "command", "command": "python \"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR\"/.claude/hooks/format_python.py" }
]
}
]
}
}
On macOS or Linux you may need python3 instead of python.

